sonix.stream/--background--

 DESCRIPTION
   A Reggae source class for Sonix sn9c102 based USB webcams, that is the
   Sweex 100K, the Macally IceCam, the Trust SpaceCam and friends, reached
   through poseidon.library. The object is an MMCLASS_STREAM with a single
   output port 0 carrying MMF_STREAM, so it has to be followed by a
   demultiplexer; the one shipped with it is sonix.demuxer, which turns the
   framed byte stream into MMF_VIDEO_RGB24 or MMF_VIDEO_GRAY8 pictures.

   The camera is claimed on MMM_Setup(), never on OM_NEW, so an application
   can build the whole pipeline before it touches the hardware, and only
   MMM_Setup() reports a camera which is not there.

   The data on port 0 is described by sonixwire.h: one SnxStreamHeader
   followed by any number of SnxFrameHeader plus picture pairs, all fields
   big endian. The stream header goes out in front of the first frame and
   tells the demuxer the geometry, the pixel format, the USB ids of the
   camera which was claimed and the sensor behind the controller. Every
   frame behind it is a SnxFrameHeader followed by one complete picture,
   uncompressed, so a decoder behind the demuxer gets the picture as it came
   out of the sensor.

   Pictures are 352x288, the only mode this controller is driven in. The
   camera delivers an interleaved Bayer pattern of one colour sample per
   pixel, which is averaged into one luminance per 2x2 cell and handed over
   as SNXF_GRAY8, or interpolated into packed RGB24, as SNXF_RGB24. Either
   way what comes out of the demuxer is a picture in the format the port
   announces, not a colour mosaic passed off as grey.

   The stream never ends by itself. MMM_Pull blocks in the USB transfer
   until the next frame has been read, and it only fails when the camera is
   unplugged or stops answering, which comes back as MMERR_IO_ERROR.

   A live camera has no length and cannot be seeked, so MMA_StreamLength is
   0, MMA_StreamSeekable is FALSE and MMM_Seek is refused with
   MMERR_NOT_SEEKABLE. The position is a running total however:
   MMA_StreamPosBytes, MMA_StreamPosFrames and MMA_StreamPosTime count what
   has been handed out so far and only ever move forward, the frame in the
   middle of being served included. They are meant to be polled, and a
   pipeline which reports a position that stands still while pictures are
   coming out of it looks like a stalled stream to anything above.

   The class specific attributes are answered on the port as well, so an
   application can read the sensor name with MMM_GetPort without going
   through OM_GET, and set the format with MMM_SetPort without going through
   OM_SET. Both take the same values and have the same restrictions as the
   object they forward to, including the refusal of a format change once the
   camera has been claimed.

   A buffer and a length are required: a NULL buffer, a port other than 0 or
   a length which is not positive is refused with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS, on
   MMM_Pull and on MMM_Peek alike.

 NEW ATTRIBUTES
   Attributes applicability:
     I - may be set at creation time.
     S - may be set on an existing object.
     G - may be get from an object.
     P - may be set for an object's port.
     Q - may be queried from an object's port.

   MMA_Sonix_VendorID    (V1)     [I.S.G], UWORD
   MMA_Sonix_ProductID   (V1)     [I.S.G], UWORD
   MMA_Sonix_Format      (V1)     [I.S.G], ULONG
   MMA_Sonix_ProductName (V1)     [..S.G], STRPTR
   MMA_Sonix_Sensor      (V1)     [..S.G], ULONG
   MMA_Sonix_SensorName  (V1)     [..S.G], STRPTR
   MMA_Sonix_Red         (V1)     [..S.G], LONG
   MMA_Sonix_Green       (V1)     [..S.G], LONG
   MMA_Sonix_Blue        (V1)     [..S.G], LONG
   MMA_Sonix_Gain        (V1)     [..S.G], LONG
   MMA_Sonix_Brightness  (V1)     [..S.G], LONG
   MMA_Sonix_Contrast    (V1)     [..S.G], LONG

 NEW METHODS
   MMM_Pull(port, buffer, length)  (V1)
   MMM_Peek(port, buffer, length)  (V1)
   MMM_Restore()  (V1)

 HISTORY
   1.0 (29.09.2026)
   - Initial revision.
   - MMA_StreamPosBytes, MMA_StreamPosFrames and MMA_StreamPosTime report a
     running total instead of 0, and MMA_StreamPosFrames is no longer the
     index of the frame in the buffer.
   - SNXF_GRAY8 averages the Bayer pattern into one luminance per 2x2 cell
     instead of passing the mosaic through as grey.
   - A format change is refused from MMM_Setup on, not from the first pull
     on, so it can no longer reallocate the frame buffer underneath a stream
     header which is being read.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Blue

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Blue (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Blue gain of the sensor, 0 to 31 on the scale of the original driver; out
   of range values are clamped. A PAS106B has a register for it and the
   value is read back from the camera. A TAS5110C1B has none, so the value
   is remembered and sent to the bridge register 0x10, which it shares with
   red. Can be set while the stream is running. It is accepted before the
   camera has been claimed as well, and takes effect once the camera is
   there.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Brightness

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Brightness (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Brightness of the sensor, 0 to 31; out of range values are clamped. The
   sensor register counts the other way round, the value sent to it is 0x1f
   minus this attribute. Only a PAS106B implements it: with a TAS5110C1B the
   call does nothing and the value is reported as 0. Can be set while the
   stream is running. It is accepted before the camera has been claimed as
   well, and takes effect once the camera is there.

 NOTES
   The value which comes back is clamped to 0 to 31 as well. The register
   counts down, so a camera answering with a value above 0x1f is reported as
   0 rather than as a negative brightness.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Contrast

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Contrast (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Contrast of the sensor, 0 to 31; out of range values are clamped. Only a
   PAS106B implements it: with a TAS5110C1B the call does nothing and the
   value is reported as 0. Can be set while the stream is running. It is
   accepted before the camera has been claimed as well, and takes effect
   once the camera is there.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Format

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Format (V1) [I.S.G], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Pixel format of the frame data, one of SNXF_GRAY8 and SNXF_RGB24. The
   default is SNXF_RGB24, which interpolates the Bayer pattern the camera
   delivers into packed RGB24. SNXF_GRAY8 averages the pattern into one
   luminance per 2x2 cell, which needs no colour interpolation and is about
   four times faster, at the price of colour. Any other value is refused
   with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS.

   The format can only be set before the camera has been claimed and is
   refused with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS afterwards. The reason is the stream
   header: it carries the format and the demuxer behind this object reads
   exactly one of those, so a change made while the stream runs would need a
   second header where a frame header is expected, and the demuxer would
   read it as a frame, see a size that matches nothing and give up. The
   camera is claimed by MMM_Setup(), so set the format on the object before
   the pipeline is set up. Until then it is accepted, the frame buffer is
   reallocated for it, and the one stream header is built with it.

 NOTES
   The value is a SNXF_* constant of sonixwire.h, not a Reggae MMF_* format.
   The format which carries the pictures over the demuxer follows from it:
   MMF_VIDEO_RGB24 for SNXF_RGB24 and MMF_VIDEO_GRAY8 for SNXF_GRAY8. The
   demuxer takes that format off the stream header, so it is on port 1 of
   the demuxer from the first pull and not only after an MMM_Setup.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Gain

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Gain (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Global gain of the sensor, 0 to 255 on the scale of the original driver;
   out of range values are clamped. Only a PAS106B implements it and only
   the top five bits of the value reach the camera, so what is read back
   afterwards is the setting divided by 8. With a TAS5110C1B the call does
   nothing and the value is reported as 15. Can be set while the stream is
   running. It is accepted before the camera has been claimed as well, and
   takes effect once the camera is there.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Green

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Green (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Green gain of the sensor, 0 to 31 on the scale of the original driver;
   out of range values are clamped. A PAS106B has two registers for it, both
   are written, and the value is read back from the camera. A TAS5110C1B has
   none, so the value is remembered and sent to the bridge register 0x11,
   halved on the way. Can be set while the stream is running. It is accepted
   before the camera has been claimed as well, and takes effect once the
   camera is there.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_ProductID

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_ProductID (V1) [I.S.G], UWORD

 FUNCTION
   USB product id the camera is looked up by, 0 by default, which matches a
   camera of any product. The selector is of no use once the camera has been
   claimed and is refused with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS from then on. The
   product id of the camera which was claimed is not reported here: it
   travels in the SnxStreamHeader, and a sonix.demuxer behind the object
   republishes it as its own MMA_Sonix_ProductID.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_ProductName

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_ProductName (V1) [..S.G], STRPTR

 FUNCTION
   USB product string of the camera which was claimed, as a pointer to a
   string which belongs to the object. The pointer is valid from creation on
   and the string is empty until a camera has been claimed, so an
   application can hand it out without checking first. Reading it after the
   camera is gone leaves the name of the last one behind.

 NOTES
   The string belongs to the object and stays valid until it is disposed. It
   must not be freed by the application.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Red

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Red (V1) [..S.G], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Red gain of the sensor, 0 to 31 on the scale of the original driver; out
   of range values are clamped. A PAS106B has a register for it and the
   value is read back from the camera. A TAS5110C1B has none, so the value
   is remembered and sent to the bridge register 0x10, which it shares with
   blue. Can be set while the stream is running. It is accepted before the
   camera has been claimed as well, and takes effect once the camera is
   there.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_Sensor

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_Sensor (V1) [..S.G], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Sensor found behind the sn9c102 controller, one of the SNXS_* values:
   SNXS_PAS106B or SNXS_TAS5110C1B. It is probed over I2C while the camera
   is claimed, and SNXS_NONE is reported before that. A camera with neither
   sensor is refused by MMM_Setup with MMERR_NO_STREAM_CLASS. The value also
   travels in the SnxStreamHeader and is republished by a sonix.demuxer
   behind the object.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_SensorName

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_SensorName (V1) [..S.G], STRPTR

 FUNCTION
   Name of the sensor found behind the controller, "PAS106B" or
   "TAS5110C1B", or "unknown" while no camera is open. Unlike
   MMA_Sonix_Sensor this is a plain string and needs no translation.

sonix.stream/MMA_Sonix_VendorID

 NAME
   MMA_Sonix_VendorID (V1) [I.S.G], UWORD

 FUNCTION
   USB vendor id the camera is looked up by, 0 by default, which matches a
   camera of any vendor. The selector is of no use once the camera has been
   claimed and is refused with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS from then on. The
   vendor id of the camera which was claimed is not reported here: it
   travels in the SnxStreamHeader, and a sonix.demuxer behind the object
   republishes it as its own MMA_Sonix_VendorID.

sonix.stream/MMA_StreamPosBytes

 NAME
   MMA_StreamPosBytes (V1) [..G], UQUAD

 FUNCTION
   Bytes of the stream handed out by MMM_Pull so far, the 32 bytes of the
   stream header included. It is a running total and only moves forward, and
   the frame which is being served right now counts as handed out up to the
   cursor, so the number never falls back to zero when a frame has been
   served to the end and the next pull has to wait for the camera.

 NOTES
   A live camera has no length, so MMA_StreamLength is 0 and this is not an
   offset into it that can be seeked to; MMM_Seek is refused with
   MMERR_NOT_SEEKABLE. The value is meant to be polled, to show how much of
   a stream a recording has taken in.

sonix.stream/MMA_StreamPosFrames

 NAME
   MMA_StreamPosFrames (V1) [..G], UQUAD

 FUNCTION
   Frames of the stream handed out by MMM_Pull in full so far. A frame
   counts once it has been served to the end, so a pull which asks for less
   than a frame does not move it, and the frame in the middle of being
   served is not counted yet.

 NOTES
   This is not the index of the frame which is in the buffer, which is what
   the frame header of the stream carries in fh_Index and which a demuxer
   republishes as its own MMA_Sonix_FrameIndex. The index of a stream starts
   again at 0 whenever the source is set up, this count does not.

sonix.stream/MMA_StreamPosTime

 NAME
   MMA_StreamPosTime (V1) [..G], UQUAD

 FUNCTION
   Microseconds since the camera was claimed, measured at the moment the
   last frame was served to the end, so it is the length of the stream up
   to the last whole frame and not the position inside the frame in flight.
   It is 0 as long as no frame has been served in full.

 NOTES
   A live camera has no length, so this is not a position which can be
   seeked to; MMM_Seek is refused with MMERR_NOT_SEEKABLE. Together with
   MMA_StreamPosFrames it gives the rate the camera is really running at,
   which is the same number a sonix.demuxer behind the object reports as
   MMA_Sonix_FrameTime.
sonix.stream/MMM_Peek

 NAME
   MMM_Peek -- Reads the stream without consuming it. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Peek, ULONG port, APTR buffer, ULONG
   length);

 FUNCTION
   Hands out the bytes the next MMM_Pull on the same port would hand out,
   without moving the read position, and reports how many of them there
   were. When fewer than length bytes are left in the current frame, the
   short count is returned; nothing is waited for to make the request
   complete.

   The class answers this itself instead of leaving it to the superclass,
   which pulls the bytes and seeks back, something a live camera cannot do.
   The recognition code of sonix.demuxer peeks the first 32 bytes of its
   source to find the stream header, so peeking a sonix.stream costs one
   frame interval of USB time and not a single byte of the stream. A port
   other than 0, a NULL buffer or a length which is not positive is refused
   with MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.
   port - number of port, must be 0.
   buffer - destination buffer for the stream bytes.
   length - amount of data to be read in *bytes*.

 RESULT
   Number of bytes peeked, secondary error information via MMA_ErrorCode.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode

sonix.stream/MMM_Pull

 NAME
   MMM_Pull -- Pulls framed picture data from the camera. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Pull, ULONG port, APTR buffer, ULONG
   length);

 FUNCTION
   Hands out the next bytes of the stream on port 0. The first 32 bytes are
   the SnxStreamHeader, everything behind it is a SnxFrameHeader followed by
   a complete picture. A pull which is asked for more than is left of the
   current frame returns the rest of it and a short count; the next call
   waits for the camera and continues behind the stream header, the header
   itself is sent once.

   The call blocks in the USB transfer for a frame interval whenever a new
   frame is needed, which is where a live source spends its time. It does
   not end by itself: the stream is only over when the camera is unplugged
   or stops answering, which comes back as MMERR_IO_ERROR. A port other than
   0, a NULL buffer or a length which is not positive is refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.
   port - number of port, must be 0, the single output port.
   buffer - destination buffer for the stream bytes.
   length - amount of data to be pulled in *bytes*.

 RESULT
   Number of bytes pulled, secondary error information via MMA_ErrorCode.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode

sonix.stream/MMM_Restore

 NAME
   MMM_Restore -- Puts back what a peek has read. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Restore);

 FUNCTION
   Does nothing and succeeds, because the MMM_Peek of this class leaves the
   read position where it was and there is nothing to put back. The class
   answers the call because the recognition code of sonix.demuxer restores
   whatever it peeked, and the inherited method would answer that with
   MMERR_NOT_SEEKABLE and leave the error behind.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.

 RESULT
   TRUE, there is no secondary error information.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode


